  turn 1 call_4e16fd501c0445e2934326dced69d239: patch writes /Volumes/aro/projects/prose_generation/enrichment/bible/work/s103/discovery/s103-20261009/103_3/luna/list.tsv\n@@\n strong\tincil\tmotif\tSBLGNT:Matt.7.24\thearing and doing\tThe commentary's good action is a life that holds together rather than collapses; Jesus compares hearing and doing his words to building a house on rock.\n+strong\tincil\tmotif\tSBLGNT:Heb.4.12\tword as a penetrating sword\tThe commentary pictures haqq as a straight thrust that reaches its mark; Hebrews describes God's word as a sword that pierces inward, making truth an active force.\n+strong\tincil\tmotif\tSBLGNT:Matt.11.30\teasy yoke and light burden\tThe commentary distinguishes a fitting load from forcing a camel beyond its strength; Jesus says his yoke is easy and his burden light.\n+strong\tincil\tmotif\tSBLGNT:1Cor.9.9\tworker and unmuzzled ox\tThe commentary joins a load-bearing animal to the worker's due share; Paul cites the law not to muzzle the threshing ox and applies it to the worker's right to support.\n strong\tincil\tmotif\tSBLGNT:Rom.12.20\tfeed an enemy\tThe commentary describes patient goodness turning hostility into intimacy; Paul applies Proverbs by telling believers to feed a hungry enemy.\n@@\n strong\tincil\tmotif\tSBLGNT:1Cor.12.26\tshared suffering and honor\tThe commentary says when one member weakens another bears the load; Paul says if one member suffers all suffer, and if one is honored all rejoice.\n strong\tincil\tmotif\tSBLGNT:Eph.4.16\tbody joined and growing\tThe commentary's connected plants leave no gaps and feed each other; Paul pictures a body joined together as each part works and builds the whole in love.\n+strong\tincil\tmotif\tSBLGNT:Rom.12.5\tmembers of one another\tThe commentary's repeated verb makes people both givers and receivers of counsel; Paul says the many are one body in Christ and individually members of one another.\n+strong\tincil\tmotif\tSBLGNT:1Pet.3.20\tfew saved through the flood\tThe commentary's exception separates a community from general human loss; Peter recalls that only a few, eight persons, were saved through the floodwaters.\n+strong\ttevrat\tmotif\tWLC:Gen.7.23	Noah and those with him survive\tThe commentary opens an exception within universal loss; Genesis says all living things were blotted out except Noah and those with him in the ark.\n+strong\ttevrat\tmotif\tWLC:Exod.18.18	a burden too heavy for one person\tThe commentary says rightful loads fit capacity and patience can mean carrying another's share; Jethro warns Moses that judging alone will exhaust both him and the people because the task is too heavy.\n+strong\ttevrat\tmotif\tWLC:Exod.18.21	share the work among capable people\tThe commentary describes communal support as each holding what another cannot; Jethro tells Moses to appoint capable leaders so the burden is distributed.\n+strong\ttevrat\tmotif\tWLC:Ps.118.27	bound offering at the altar\tThe commentary's sabr image includes an animal held for death; the psalm's festival instruction binds the offering with cords to the altar's horns.\n+strong\ttevrat\tmotif\tWLC:1Kgs.18.45	cloud becomes heavy rain\tThe commentary links rain-bearing clouds to vegetation; after Elijah sees a small cloud, the sky darkens and a great rain falls, a specific cloud-to-rain sequence.\n+strong\ttevrat\tmotif\tWLC:Gen.6.9	Noah righteous in a corrupt generation\tThe commentary's exception identifies those who escape the general human condition; Genesis calls Noah righteous and blameless among his contemporaries before the flood.\n@@\n medium\tincil\tmotif\tSBLGNT:Matt.20.8\twages at day's end\tThe commentary connects work to the day's wage; the vineyard owner pays the workers at evening, giving a narrative setting for labor, time, and compensation.\n+medium\tincil\tmotif\tSBLGNT:Matt.6.4	reward for hidden good work\tThe commentary observes that this ayah names mutual counsel instead of a wage; Jesus says the Father will repay the person who gives alms in secret, an alternate framing of good action.\n+medium\tincil\tmotif\tSBLGNT:Matt.11.28	rest for the burdened\tThe commentary pictures sabr as staying under hardship and haqq as a load scaled to capacity; Jesus invites the weary and burdened to come for rest.\n+medium\tincil\tmotif\tSBLGNT:Luke.6.38	generous measure returned\tThe commentary sets an unweighed heap beside reward without reckoning; Jesus describes a measure pressed down and overflowing, linking giving to the measure returned.\n@@\n medium\ttevrat\tmotif\tWLC:Gen.15.6\tAbraham trusts a promise\tThe commentary hears belief as reliance that gives security; Abraham trusts God's promise of descendants, and that trust is reckoned to him as righteousness.\n+medium\ttevrat\tmotif\tWLC:Gen.6.9	Noah's righteousness\tThe commentary describes a group set apart from humanity's loss by its defining acts; Genesis presents Noah as righteous and blameless in his generation, a related exception profile.\n@@\n medium\ttevrat\tmotif\tWLC:Exod.17.12\tothers hold up a weary leader\tThe commentary describes sabr as one person standing behind another when they weaken; Aaron and Hur support Moses' hands until sunset so Israel can continue the fight.\n+medium\ttevrat\tmotif\tWLC:Exod.26.6\tjoined parts make one dwelling\tThe commentary describes fitting pieces and connected vegetation; Exodus joins the tabernacle curtains with clasps so the dwelling becomes one whole.\n@@\n medium\ttevrat\tmotif\tMishnah Avot 2:16\twork that one must not abandon\tThe commentary describes patience as holding steady until work bears fruit; Avot says one need not finish the work but is not free to desist from it.\n@@\n medium\ttevrat\tmotif\tSirach 2:4\ttrust and patience in trial\tThe commentary defines sabr as holding firm when hardship has no exit; Sirach tells the one serving God to accept what comes and be patient when humbled.\n@@\n-medium\ttevrat\tmotif\tWLC:Gen.15.6\tfaith credited as righteousness\tThe commentary's first qualification is belief; Genesis gives a concrete instance in Abraham, whose trust in God's promise is counted as righteousness.\n-medium\ttevrat\tmotif\tWLC:Exod.17.12\tlabor's cost and endurance\tThe commentary links work, time, and patience through hardship; Jacob's account of heat, frost, and sleepless service gives a lived scene of endurance within labor.\n medium\ttevrat\tmotif\tWLC:Deut.4.8\trighteous statutes\tThe commentary's haqq means right that is also binding; Moses asks what nation has statutes and judgments as righteous as Israel's, giving a concrete scriptural form of binding right.\n@@\n-medium\tincil\tmotif\tSBLGNT:Did.4.1\tword remembered day and night\tThe commentary emphasizes repeated advice and the joining of day to night; the Didache tells a learner to remember the one who speaks God's word day and night.\n-medium\tincil\tmotif\tSBLGNT:Did.4.2\tseek the words of the saints\tThe commentary pictures each person as both recipient and giver of counsel; the Didache urges believers to seek the faces of the saints daily and rest on their words.\n+medium\tincil\tmotif\tDidache 4.1\tword remembered day and night\tThe commentary emphasizes repeated advice and the joining of day to night; the Didache tells a learner to remember the one who speaks God's word day and night.\n+medium\tincil\tmotif\tDidache 4.2\tseek the words of the saints\tThe commentary pictures each person as both recipient and giver of counsel; the Didache urges believers to seek the faces of the saints daily and rest on their words.\n medium\tincil\tmotif\t1 Clement 38.1-2\tstrong care for weak\tThe commentary's sabr includes a guarantor who stands with others in their affairs; Clement says the strong should care for the weak and the weak should respect the strong.\n@@\n-medium\tincil\tmotif\tSBLGNT:1Cor.3.9\tfellow workers\tThe commentary's reciprocal counsel makes a community work together; Paul calls its members God's coworkers, not isolated laborers.\n weak\ttevrat\tsoydas\tWLC:Gen.15.6\tאמנ; table sound correspondence, unverified\tThe commentary's âmanû means belief heard as trust; Hebrew he'emin describes Abraham trusting God's promise, a semantic match for the table's unverified sound correspondence.\n@@\n-weak\ttevrat\tsoydas\tTargum Onkelos on Genesis 15:6\tAramaic אמנ; table sound correspondence, unverified\tThe commentary hears iman as trust; Onkelos renders Abraham's belief as trust in the Lord's Memra and reckons it as merit, an Aramaic root connection that remains unverified.\n-weak\ttevrat\tsoydas\tWLC:Eccl.2.22\tעמל; table sound correspondence, unverified\tThe commentary associates work with a due wage and an escape from loss; Qohelet asks what gain comes from toil, a specific contrary outcome for the possible עמל correspondence.\n+medium\ttevrat\tyorum_gelenegi\tTargum Onkelos on Genesis 15:6\tMemra and merit\tOnkelos renders Abraham's trust as belief in the Lord's Memra and reckons it as merit; this Jewish interpretation makes the commentary's trust in a promise explicitly mediated through God's word.\n*** End Patch
